Id: sum003w
Severity: WARN
Category: boot
Check: check_single
Fix: Remove SYSTEMD_SULOGIN_FORCE from the rescue and emergency units (or --force from the inittab's sulogin line), or give root a password.
References: sulogin(8); systemd.special(7); https://systemd.io/ENVIRONMENT/
Controls: CIS 4.1; NIST AC-3, IA-2; ISO 8.5, 8.9; CE Secure configuration

Single-user mode runs sulogin, but told to let root in without a
password when root's password is locked, and root's password is locked
(! or * before it in /etc/shadow, as Ubuntu installs it, and as Debian
does when sudo is chosen). sulogin -e (--force) does that, and so does
systemd-sulogin-shell, which systemd's rescue.service and
emergency.service run, when SYSTEMD_SULOGIN_FORCE=1 is in their
environment. Anyone at the console who can reach rescue or emergency
mode (the kernel command line, or a boot that fails) then gets a root
shell without any password.

The setting is often added to get past "the root account is locked" in
emergency mode. Remove it and use sudo from a working boot, or a boot
loader password and a rescue medium, to repair a broken system; or set
a root password, which sulogin then asks for. Without read access to
/etc/shadow (not running as root) it is not reported.
